## Q3

It seems possible that the number of people on the trail on any particular day could be impacted by many things in this data set. Let's explore how the high temperature (hightemp) and whether or not it rains (this will be a new column we make) relates to the average number of users on the trail.

----

##### 3.1
```{r}
# 3.1
rt <- RailTrail %>% 
  mutate(rain = ifelse(precip > 0, 1, 0))
```

##### 3.2
```{r}
# 3.2
palette(c("orange","skyblue"))

plot(volume ~ hightemp, data=rt, col=as.factor(rain), main="RailTrail Data Set")

legend("topleft", legend=c("No Rain", "Rain"), col=palette(), pch=16)
```

##### 3.3
Only the estimate for `hightemp` was significant.
```{r}
# 3.3
mylm1 <- lm(volume ~ hightemp + rain + hightemp:rain, data = rt)
summary(mylm1) %>% pander()
```

##### 3.4
Let's remove the more complicated term `hightemp:rain` and see if the other terms are now significant.
```{r}
# 3.4
mylm2 <- lm(volume ~ hightemp + rain, data = rt)
summary(mylm2) %>% pander()
```

##### 3.5
Now let's plot a new graph.
```{r}
# 3.5
palette(c("orange","skyblue"))
b <- coef(mylm2)

  plot(volume ~ hightemp, data=rt, col=as.factor(rain), main="RailTrail Data Set")
  legend("topleft", legend=c("No Rain", "Rain"), col=palette(), pch=16)
  curve(b[1] + b[2]*x, add=TRUE, lwd=2, col="orange")
  curve(b[1] + b[3] + b[2]*x, add=TRUE, lwd=2, col="skyblue")
```

##### 3.6
Let's run the diagnostic plots to see if a linear regression model seems fitting for the data.
```{r}
# 3.6
par(mfrow=c(1,3))
plot(mylm2,which=1)
qqPlot(mylm2$residuals, id=FALSE)
plot(mylm2$residuals)

par(mfrow=c(1,1))
```

